#include <signal.h>

int main(int argc, char * argv[]) {

        kill(0, SIGPIPE);

}


The program makes no sense, but it illustrates the problem I'm seeing: when
compiling using the Compaq C++ compiler and `-std strict_ansi', the
signal.h in /usr/lib/cmplrs/cxx/V6.5-041/include is eventually including
signal_cname_impl, and only the stripped-down set of signals from that
header file are visible (SIGPIPE and SIGCHLD, among many others, are not
defined).

In addition, certain functions that would be declared in the C signal.h
aren't present, specifically kill().

Can anyone explain why `-std strict_ansi' has such a stripped down set of
defines and declarations when signal.h is included?  

